	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: The primary outcome was grade 3 primary graft dysfunction within 72 hours after lung transplantation, defined according to the ISHLT consensus criteria using PaO2/FiO2 below 200, chest-radiograph infiltrates, and/or ECMO at 72 hours. In the independent temporal external-validation cohort, Random Forest achieved AUC 0.8214, accuracy 0.7654, sensitivity 0.8235, specificity 0.6667, and F1-score 0.8155. AUC confidence intervals, PPV, and NPV were not reported. Calibration curves and decision-curve analysis were presented and described as supporting calibration and clinical usefulness.

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: The study enrolled lung transplant recipients from a defined single-center period, but excluded 56 of 710 derivation-period patients and 16 of 183 validation-period patients for severe missing data, without comparing included and excluded patients. The effect of these exclusions on cohort representativeness is therefore unclear.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The nine final predictors were routinely collected recipient and perioperative variables available by completion of transplantation and before the 72-hour PGD3 endpoint. Definitions and measurement categories were reported, and feature selection was performed using the training cohort.
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: PGD3 was defined and graded according to the latest ISHLT consensus criteria at prespecified T0, T24, T48, and T72 time points using oxygenation, radiographic infiltrates, and ECMO support.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: The study used a separate training cohort, internal validation, later temporal external validation, cross-validated hyperparameter tuning, calibration assessment, DCA, and explainability analyses. However, it was not explicitly stated that K-nearest-neighbour imputation was fitted only within training data, no AUC confidence intervals were reported, and external validation was temporal but from the same center.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly evaluates interpretable machine-learning models for predicting grade 3 primary graft dysfunction after lung transplantation using routinely collected clinical and perioperative variables. It is directly applicable to AI research in thoracic organ transplantation.

